+touch zero
+cat <<EOI >=1kb
A deflate stream compresses what is written to it into another stream and
a deflate input stream decompresses what it reads from another stream. The
compressed data is written as it fills the compressor's blocks, so a writer
may produce a lot of output before anything at all reaches the other
stream, and only closing the stream writes the final block. A reader gets
the decompressed data as soon as the decompressor produces it, and if it
is told that the compressed data ends the stream, it verifies that
nothing follows it. Otherwise, it leaves whatever follows
the compressed data in the other stream, for the reader to read next. The
streams buffer the data on both sides, so writing and reading a character
at a time costs no more than writing and reading it in larger parts, and
neither stream depends on the other: what one writes, any decompressor can
read, and what the other reads, any compressor can write, as long as it is
deflate data.
EOI
+cat 1kb 1kb 1kb 1kb 1kb 1kb 1kb 1kb >=8kb
+cat 8kb 8kb 8kb 8kb 8kb 8kb 8kb 8kb >=64kb
+$* -c 6 <:'hello' >=hello
+cat <:'tail' >=tail
+cat hello tail >=hello-tail

w = $~

: round-trip
: Compress into a stream and decompress from a stream
:
{{
  $* -c 6 <<<$w/zero | $* -d >>>$w/zero : zero
  $* -c 6 <<<$w/1kb  | $* -d >>>$w/1kb  : 1kb
  $* -c 6 <<<$w/64kb | $* -d >>>$w/64kb : 64kb
  $* -c 0 <<<$w/64kb | $* -d >>>$w/64kb : store
  $* -c 9 <<<$w/64kb | $* -d >>>$w/64kb : smallest
}}

: rest-left
: Data followed by more of the stream, which is left in it
:
$* -d --rest <<<$w/hello-tail >:'hello|tail'

: junk-rejected
: Data followed by more of the stream that must end with it
:
$* -d <<<$w/hello-tail 2>>EOE != 0
error: junk after deflate data
EOE

: incomplete-rejected
: Stream that ends before the data does
:
$* -d <<<$w/zero 2>>EOE != 0
error: incomplete deflate data
EOE
